# FIVE COMPUTERS, THREE DISPLAYS, AND AN ASCENT -- the narrated version of
# 4gpc-ops1-plus-gpc5.script, for recording.  Run from discretePanel/:
#
#   python3 simulatePASS.py --gpcs 1-5 --crts 3 --size 384 --tape ~/workspace/pass-run/OI340700-v44boot.mmv --layout examples/5gpc-3crt-subtitled.layout --script examples/5gpc-3crt-subtitled.script --duration 2400
#
# --layout is what starts subtitles.py; without it the captions below go
# nowhere and the run is simply the unnarrated one.
#
# --size 384 IS NOT OPTIONAL, though --scale is: scale already defaults to
# 0.8, but size defaults to 512, and the layout beside this file records
# window positions taken at 384.  Ask for 512 and the windows will not sit
# where the layout puts them.
#
# WHAT IS BEING SHOWN.  The orbiter's real ascent arrangement: four General
# Purpose Computers flying as ONE redundant set, executing the same software
# on the same inputs in lockstep, and a fifth computer that is NOT part of it
# and drives its own display.  On the vehicle that fifth machine runs the
# Backup Flight System; we have no BFS, so it stays in OPS 0 -- still a fifth
# computer on every bus, still in the common set, visibly running something
# else.
#
# THE THREE THINGS A VIEWER SHOULD COME AWAY WITH, and the script is paced to
# make each visible rather than merely true:
#
#   1. Each computer is loaded from a tape, one at a time, by hand.  There is
#      no operating system until somebody flips switches.
#   2. Ascent cannot be commanded from a cold machine.  OPS 0 -> OPS 901 ->
#      OPS 101 is the vehicle's own sequence and the flight software enforces
#      it: DM6OPS's transition table simply does not list 2101 as reachable
#      from OPS 0.  The script goes through G9 because it must.
#   3. A display belongs to whichever computer the crew gives it to, and a
#      GPC runs up to three major functions at once -- so the same computer
#      can show an ascent trajectory on one screen and a memory display on
#      another, which is exactly what CRT3 demonstrates at the end.
#
# EVERY WAIT IN HERE IS A CONDITION, NOT A GUESS.  The IPLs are gated on the
# GPCIPL menu appearing and on the MODE talkback reading RUN, so a slow host
# stretches the recording without ever desynchronising the captions from what
# is on screen.  The only fixed delays are between a caption and the action
# it describes, and the reading time of a caption that follows one.  The two
# OPS loads are gated on the display changing and on the ascent format
# announcing itself by name, so the narration cannot run ahead of the
# vehicle.

+0     subtitle Space Shuttle avionics: five computers, three displays.\nWe are going to fly an ascent configuration.
+6     subtitle Everything you are about to see is done from the crew panels,\nexactly as it was done on the flight deck.

# --- all five GPCs to HALT, the three IDPs on --------------------------------
+5     subtitle First, all five computers to HALT. Nothing is running yet.
+3     gpc 1
+0     mode HALT
+0.5   gpc 2
+0.5   mode HALT
+0.5   gpc 3
+0.5   mode HALT
+0.5   gpc 4
+0.5   mode HALT
+0.5   gpc 5
+0.5   mode HALT

+1     subtitle Power up the three display units.
+2     idppower 1 on
+0.5   idppower 2 on
+0.5   idppower 3 on
+1     subtitle All three start on the GNC major function.\nCRT3 will be moved later, and that move matters.
+3     majfunc 1 gnc
+0.5   majfunc 2 gnc
+0.5   majfunc 3 gnc

# --- the four that will form the redundant set -------------------------------
+3     subtitle Now load each computer from the mass memory tape.\nGPC1 first, onto CRT1.
+4     script ipl-one-gpc-subtitled.script gpc=1 crt=1 idp=1 kb=KB1

+2     subtitle GPC2, GPC3 and GPC4 follow, sharing CRT2.
+4     script ipl-one-gpc-subtitled.script gpc=2 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2
+2     script ipl-one-gpc-subtitled.script gpc=3 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2
+2     script ipl-one-gpc-subtitled.script gpc=4 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2

# --- then GPC5, onto CRT3, which is also how IDP3 gets its background --------
+2     subtitle Last, GPC5 -- the one that will NOT join the set.\nIt loads onto CRT3.
+4     subtitle A display is given its background only by the computer\nthat loads it, so GPC5 must be the one to load CRT3.
+5     kybdsel right 3
+2     script ipl-one-gpc-subtitled.script gpc=5 crt=3 idp=3 kb=KB2
+2     kybdsel right 2

+2     subtitle Five computers loaded. All five talkbacks read RUN.\nNone of them is flying anything yet.

# --- take CRT3 out of both NBATs --------------------------------------------
+5     subtitle Before asking for an OPS, tell the software which computer\nowns which bus. That table is the NBAT.
+6     subtitle <right>The tape assigns CRT3 to GPC3 in BOTH configurations\nwe are about to use. We must take it back, twice.
+6     subtitle <right>First for memory configuration 9.
+3     keys ITEM 1 + 9 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 1 4 + 0 EXEC

+3     subtitle <right>Now memory configuration 1 -- ascent.\nFour computers, four flight-critical strings.
+4     keys ITEM 1 + 1 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 2 + 1 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 3 + 2 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 4 + 3 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 5 + 4 EXEC
+2     subtitle <right>GPC1 to string 1, GPC2 to 2, GPC3 to 3, GPC4 to 4.
+4     keys ITEM 7 + 1 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 8 + 2 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 9 + 3 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 1 0 + 4 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 1 1 + 0 EXEC
+2     subtitle <right>CRT1 to GPC1, CRT2 to GPC2, and CRT3 to nobody.
+4     keys ITEM 1 2 + 1 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 1 3 + 2 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 1 4 + 0 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 1 8 + 1 EXEC
+5     keys ITEM 1 9 + 2 EXEC

+3     subtitle Move CRT3 off GNC, so the ascent software has no claim on it.
+4     majfunc 3 pl

# --- OPS 0 -> OPS 901 -> OPS 101 --------------------------------------------
+4     subtitle Now the transition. Ascent cannot be commanded from a cold\nmachine -- the software will not allow it.
+7     subtitle <left>So we go by way of OPS 9, as the countdown does.
+4     keys OPS 9 0 1 PRO
+4     subtitle Four computers are loading memory configuration 9 together.

# WAIT FOR THE MAJOR MODE, WHICH IS THE ONLY THING THAT CHANGES.  Both of
# the obvious conditions are met INSTANTLY and are therefore no wait at all:
#
#   'new-screen' is any page change, and the page changes at once
#   'title GPC MEMORY' is what OPS 0 ALREADY shows
#
# G9's display is called GPC MEMORY too -- the difference is the major mode
# in the corner, 0001 before and 9011 after.  Measured the hard way on
# 2026-09-23: with 'new-screen' the wait was met after 0.1 s, OPS 101 was
# keyed ELEVEN seconds after OPS 901 instead of the ninety the unnarrated
# script allows, the ascent request went into a machine still loading G9 and
# was lost, and the run sat at the LAUNCH TRAJ wait until it timed out with
# CRT1 reading "9011/ / GPC MEMORY 1".
wait crt 1 title "9011" timeout 600

+1     subtitle <left>And now ascent: OPS 101.
+6     keys OPS 1 0 1 PRO
+4     subtitle Watch CRT1 and CRT2. Four computers, one redundant set,\nloading the ascent software in lockstep.

# The ascent display announces itself by name, so wait for the name.
wait crt 1 title "LAUNCH TRAJ" timeout 600

# --- and finally, give CRT3 to GPC5 -----------------------------------------
+2     subtitle LAUNCH TRAJ 1. The set is flying an ascent.
+6     subtitle But look at CRT3 -- it is showing GPC MEMORY, driven by GPC1.\nNot by GPC5.
+7     subtitle A display with no owner goes to the lowest-numbered computer\nin THAT major function, and in PL that is GPC1.
+8     subtitle Which is why GPC1 is showing two different screens at once:\nascent on CRT1, memory on CRT3. One computer, two major functions.
+9     subtitle <right>So we hand CRT3 to GPC5 explicitly.
+5     kybdsel right 3
+2     keys KB2 GPC/CRT 5 3 EXEC
+4     kybdsel right 2

+3     subtitle There: CRT3 now reads 5.
+4     subtitle Four computers flying an ascent as one machine,\nand a fifth alongside them, outside the set, on its own display.
+8     subtitle That is the configuration the orbiter launched in --\nwith the Backup Flight System in the fifth seat.
+8     subtitle
